<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Doctrine mapping for `AttributeOption`, the Catalog context's third aggregate root (ADR-0017
    decision 2). This is ADR-0004's original `attribute_option` table sketch, unamended.

    `attribute_id` is a plain typed column: no `<many-to-one>`, no FK (ADR-0009 decision 4). Deleting
    an attribute sweeps its options with a set-based DELETE in the same transaction (C-20, plan D7),
    not an `ON DELETE CASCADE`.

    WHY `label_key` AND NOT AN EXPRESSION INDEX ON `lower(label)` (plan D3). C-17 is "option labels
    are unique within an attribute, case-insensitively". DBAL cannot declare an expression index in a
    mapping, and its PostgreSQL introspection reads one back as a different index (the expression's
    `attnum` is 0), so a hand-written `(attribute_id, lower(label))` would be re-diffed by every
    `make migration.make` forever — the partial-index footgun, but with no spelling that fixes it. A
    generated column has the same problem. So the aggregate stores the case-folded form itself:
    `label_key` = `AttributeLabel::key()`, assigned in the same statement as `label`, and the index is
    a plain two-column one DBAL round-trips. PHP's `mb_strtolower` is then the only case-folder, in
    the pre-check and the stored key alike: no PHP-vs-Postgres collation drift.

    WHY THERE IS NO `position` (plan D3). Option order is insertion order is `ORDER BY id`: ids are
    UUIDv7 (timestamp first, compared bytewise by Postgres), minted monotonically, and every insert is
    serialised by `CategoryTreeLock`. ponytail: the ceiling is a backwards wall-clock step between two
    separate adds; the upgrade is an additive `position` column backfilled from id order, which
    manual reordering would need anyway.

    HOW THE INDEX WIDENS FOR DEPENDENT OPTIONS (plan D14). When Model options hang under Brand
    options, "Standard" must be legal under two brands, so uniqueness becomes per (attribute, parent
    option). That replaces this index with the slice-1 two-partial-index pair:
    `(attribute_id, label_key) WHERE (parent_option_id IS NULL)` and
    `(attribute_id, parent_option_id, label_key) WHERE (parent_option_id IS NOT NULL)`. One plain
    three-column index would not do, because NULLs are distinct (the C-3 lesson). No
    `parent_option_id` exists yet, on purpose.

    The index's leading column also serves `countForAttribute()`, `removeAllForAttribute()` and the
    per-attribute option list.
-->
<doctrine-mapping xmlns="http://doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping"
                  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                  xsi:schemaLocation="http://doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping
                                      https://www.doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping.xsd">

    <entity name="App\Domain\Catalog\Entity\AttributeOption" table="catalog_attribute_option">

        <unique-constraints>
            <!-- C-17: option labels unique within an attribute, case-insensitively, via `label_key`. -->
            <unique-constraint name="uniq_catalog_attribute_option_label" columns="attribute_id,label_key"/>
        </unique-constraints>

        <id name="id" type="catalog_attribute_option_id" column="id">
            <generator strategy="NONE"/>
        </id>

        <!-- Reference by identity, not by association — see the header. -->
        <field name="attributeId" column="attribute_id" type="catalog_attribute_id" nullable="false"/>

        <field name="label" column="label" type="catalog_attribute_label" length="100" nullable="false"/>
        <!-- `mb_strtolower` never lengthens a string past its `mb_strlen`, so 100 suffices. -->
        <field name="labelKey" column="label_key" type="string" length="100" nullable="false"/>

    </entity>

</doctrine-mapping>
